            <ArticleTitle>Assessment of risk factors for coronary artery disease in divisions of internal medicine. The FAPOI Study Group. Federazione delle Associazioni dei Primari Ospedalieri Internisti.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>147-52</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine the prevalences and clustering of risk factors for coronary artery disease among unselected patients admitted to the first level of hospitalization.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The survey was prospectively designed to review all the medical records of patients discharged in the week starting at 0800 h on 21 October 1996. Family and personal histories of common risk factors, major routine biochemical data at entry, treatments during hospitalization and diagnoses at discharge (ICD-9 codes) were examined. In total 235 divisions of internal medicine uniformly distributed throughout Italy took part in the survey.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The survey shows that unselected patients admitted to divisions of internal medicine are characterized by a high prevalence of risk factors for coronary artery disease. Some of these are incompletely assessed. Definition of the clustering of risk factors is also far from optimal.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The risk profiles of unselected patients at cardiovascular risk hospitalized in divisions of internal medicine are incompletely assessed. Several reasons may prevent a thorough detection of risk factors and consequently their treatment. There is much potential for improvement.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
